BT /F1 10 Tf 14 TL 54 738 Td (Notice Standards in Public Records Disclosure: A Cross-Jurisdictional Review \(SAMPLE,) Tj T* (synthetic demo paper\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Abstract) Tj T* (This paper examines how courts and legislatures in several invented jurisdictions have) Tj T* (treated the duty to give notice before public records are disclosed. We combine a doctrinal) Tj T* (review of leading decisions with a survey of 40 records officers about how notice duties) Tj T* (work in daily practice. We find that notice standards are applied inconsistently, that older) Tj T* (authorities are still cited after later decisions limited them, and that digital notice) Tj T* (systems, including counterfeit consciousness tools used to classify requests, are adopted) Tj T* (faster than the law can adapt.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Keywords: records disclosure, notice, administrative law) Tj T* () Tj T* (1. Introduction) Tj T* (Public bodies must give affected persons notice before releasing records. The leading) Tj T* (authority is Harlow v. Zarnell Services \(1\), which held that a single posted notice was) Tj T* (enough. Later courts have questioned that approach, and the reasoning in Tavren v. Pemberton) Tj T* (Holdings \(16\) has been described as unclear on what counts as adequate delivery. By) Tj T* (contrast, Darnel v. Orlath Trading \(28\) remains widely followed on the requirement of timely) Tj T* (service. Another case, Fairbanks v. Quillon Group, is often cited for the proposition that) Tj T* (electronic notice is sufficient. Statutory duties are set by the Brightmoor Civic Records) Tj T* (Act 221, read with the Northmark Records Administration Regulation 271. The Records) Tj T* (Disclosure Act 2011 is also relevant to the discussion that follows [1][2][9].) Tj T* () Tj T* (2. Methods) Tj T* (We reviewed reported decisions and statutes in three jurisdictions and surveyed 40 records) Tj T* (officers about their notice practices. Survey responses were coded by one researcher.) Tj T* (Participants were recruited through professional networks.) Tj T* () Tj T* (3. Results) Tj T* (Table 1 summarises the survey. Most respondents \(31 of 40\) said notice is usually given by) Tj T* (electronic message, while 9 relied on posted notices. Figure 1 shows the spread of reported) Tj T* (delivery times, which ranged from same-day to more than two weeks [3][4].) Tj T* () Tj T* (4. Discussion) Tj T* (The doctrinal review suggests that adequacy of notice depends heavily on the forum. Where a) Tj T* (statute is silent, courts fall back on general fairness standards. Our survey results are) Tj T* (consistent with that reading, although the sample is small and drawn from professional) Tj T* (networks.) Tj T* () Tj T* (5. Conclusion) Tj T* (Notice duties for records disclosure are unsettled. Legislatures should set clear delivery) Tj T* (standards, and courts should state plainly which earlier authorities they no longer follow.) Tj T* () Tj T* (References) Tj T* ([1] Aldrin, P. \(1998\). Records and the public interest. Journal of Administrative Practice,) Tj T* (12\(3\), 44-61.) Tj T* ET
